WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.650
What are the most challenging economics

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.060
of the magazine industry?

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.490
Was the goal to reinvent
the business model?

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:09.870
Certainly that's the goal.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.100
I think, you know,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.900
most small independent
magazine businesses are folding

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.080
because they're not
thinking outside the box.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.780
Time is of the essence.
There's so much going on.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.520
How can you fight for people's attention?

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.380
What can we make that will actually break

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.550
through and matter?

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.620
Karlie Kloss is many things,

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.685
but let's start with supermodel.

00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:38.040
Karlie!
A businesswoman.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.340
I invest in companies and
ideas and people I believe in.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.510
Money is power.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:47.940
And she's in the headlines
whether she wants them or not.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.480
You and Josh are very private
people. Is that intentional?

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.980
It is because we really
care about our real life.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.660
I think nobody else
deserves access to that.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.480
Did it change when
President Trump was elected? No.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.580
Long before that as a model

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:07.170
I think it was definitely
a line I had to walk.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.680
Now in her biggest bet yet
she's going from covergirl

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.860
to medium mogul, buying
up iconic magazines

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:14.970
as the industry fights

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.060
to survive in a world
dominated by digital media.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.550
I think about it like
we're running a startup

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.530
inside a vintage car.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.080
So how does a woman who's
graced over a hundred covers

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.230
aim to reinvent this treasured
but struggling format

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.360
and keep reinventing herself?

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.490
So what kind of kid were you? Were you the

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.140
wallflower or the bookworm?

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.850
The queen bee? I was a

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.850
total nerd.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.010
Obviously we need your yearbook.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.830
I need to meet your
teachers, your prom date.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.350
To find out, we're entering her world.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.490
A life built in New York City
that all began in the Midwest.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.920
You are on the cover of all of these.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:50.970
Oh, and the, there's many,

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.290
many more, but we've don't have time.

00:02:15.205 --> 00:02:16.735
So this is your
high school.

00:02:16.735 --> 00:02:18.660
This is Webster Groves High School.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.180
I imagine this brings back memories.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.500
Oh, so many memories.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.540
You're a nineties baby. You're down here.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.910
I mean hopefully.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:33.660
Although I think I was in China
doing a photo shoot on the

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:34.890
Great Wall for Vogue.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.960
As one does.
I would be in here.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.220
So you were on the Great Wall

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.740
of China while everyone
else was in the gym.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:41.970
Yes. Taking a photo. But

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.740
that's also just such a perfect example of

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:44.573
how you held these two worlds.

00:02:44.573 --> 00:02:45.406
Oh it was my life. I
wouldn't want it any other way.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.620
Kloss's modeling career
happened basically overnight,

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.500
but back in high school she
was a self-proclaimed nerdy

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.350
teenager just trying
to get a date to prom.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:02.285
Is it true that you
rejected her? Oh well you

00:03:02.285 --> 00:03:03.285
rejected is
a

00:03:03.285 --> 00:03:04.295
strong word.

00:03:04.295 --> 00:03:06.195
Karlie is that
how you would put it?

00:03:06.195 --> 00:03:09.160
That is how I would.
You did end up going

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.600
to prom together senior year.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.455
Yes we did. And you did end
up wearing Dior. I saw that.

00:03:13.455 --> 00:03:16.960
I did end up wearing Dior
Couture. So you, box checked.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.350
It was beautiful and it was
orange and I got my tuxedo from

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:21.350
Men's Warehouse.

00:03:29.615 --> 00:03:32.110
Hello. My
parents'

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:33.110
home.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:33.943
Welcome. Thank you to your mom

00:03:33.943 --> 00:03:36.910
and dad I should say, for having us here.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:42.730
What was the biggest
culture shock when you went

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.050
into the modeling world?

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:48.250
I think I just was always
authentically who I am,

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:52.990
which is a girl from the Midwest
until I was 13 years old.

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.660
I never flew on an
airplane, like my family

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:58.840
and I drove cross-country
for family vacations.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:02.620
So fast forward to when I'm 15 years old

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:07.030
and I have a Cinderella story,
truly overnight success.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:10.180
I walk in the Calvin Klein
New York Fashion Week show

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.970
and it just puts me on the
map in such a crazy way.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:16.780
And so quite quickly at 15
I was jet setting between,

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:18.220
you know the suburbs of St.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.280
Louis and Paris, Tokyo, New York.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.480
Oh wow. They cut a circle into square

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:29.480
slices.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:32.500
You didn't know math would allow
it. Here we are, mom, come.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.650
Okay. Hi mom. I'm introducing
her to Emo's Pizza.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.510
As a mom, like what was it like

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.250
for you sending your daughter off

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:42.190
to the runway when she was a teenager?

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:43.570
It was exciting.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:44.980
It was such a learning experience

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:47.380
and the world became your classroom.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:52.180
I imagine you made a
lot of money very fast.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.440
How did you deal with that?

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.450
I started working so young

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.970
and was so fortunate
to sort of make income

00:04:57.970 --> 00:04:59.470
that I could have never dreamt of

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:02.050
and it really happened
around the same time

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:03.700
of the '08 crisis

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.740
and my family was really impacted

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:08.530
as many families in this country were,

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:10.660
and we went through bankruptcy

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:13.510
and we lost, you know,
the roof over our head

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:16.480
and it was like, it was an
incredibly painful period.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.250
I also was beginning my career

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:20.200
and was able to support my family,

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.760
support my sisters through school.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.290
And so for me my career
was a, a way to be able

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:29.050
to like support the
people I cared most about.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:34.030
My mom saved like newspaper clippings

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:36.040
and all the things
through all the years. You

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.290
are on the cover of all of these.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.810
Oh, and there's many, many
more, but we don't have time.

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:43.503
That doesn't to

00:05:43.503 --> 00:05:44.860
me even look like you.
It doesn't even look

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:45.860
like me, right?

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:48.640
No. Can I tell you that's
what being a great model is,

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.290
is that you, you transform.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:54.100
I started modeling at a period
where like you were seen

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.740
and not heard to then
the dawn of social media

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:58.550
where you can speak your truth,

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:00.110
you can build your name into a brand,

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.150
and models became celebrities.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:03.620
It started to become Karlie

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.680
and what she has to say, what she's doing,

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:07.730
what she stands for.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:10.010
Yeah. Hi guys, I'm Karlie Kloss

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:12.260
and welcome to my YouTube channel.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:13.400
With social media,

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.430
suddenly Kloss had a
platform beyond the runway.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.080
It's New York Fashion Week

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.785
and I'm on my way to Michael Kors.

00:06:22.785 --> 00:06:25.280
She launched
her own YouTube channel at the

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.140
dawn of the creator economy.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:29.360
I'm in love. It's perfect.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.790
Started her first
business, Karlie's Kookies,

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:34.460
which funded over half a
million school lunches.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.640
Took a life-changing coding
course at the Flatiron School

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:41.630
and in 2019 stepped into
Heidi Klum's iconic role as host

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:42.710
of Project Runway.

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.430
Off Camera. Kloss was making bets on

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:48.740
where culture was moving, backing a range

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:49.850
of budding startups.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.450
What I do with my capital
is I invest in companies

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:55.820
and ideas and people I believe in.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:59.330
I support issues that I care deeply about,

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:01.040
like money is power.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:02.360
Was there a moment

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:06.590
where you decided I don't
wanna be a model forever

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:09.200
and that you wanted to have
a career beyond modeling?

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.220
Yeah, I think since I started
this career I've always been

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:15.380
thinking about what comes after.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.350
For a long time, I thought
of it as a means to an end

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:21.800
to be able to make money and
then also to see the world

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.140
and not only that, being the ateliers

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:28.070
of the most brilliant
creatives in the industry.

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.920
I love walking runways.
You don't turn that off.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:33.080
It's a part of who I am,

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.600
but I think it's never
been all of who I am.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:38.150
You've bought Life Magazine,

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:41.240
you bought the fashion
magazine i-D from Vice.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.390
You led a group of
investors to buy W Magazine.

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:46.700
What's the grand vision for Bedford Media?

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:50.450
Bedford is sort of taking
this startup approach

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:52.730
to these sort of heritage brands.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:54.860
I mean, Life is 90 years old.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:57.350
I think about it like
we're running a startup

00:07:57.350 --> 00:07:59.570
inside a vintage car.

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:03.890
Independent small media
should continue to exist.

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:06.920
I have an incredible
editor-in-chief at i-D

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:09.500
and the editorial team
and the commercial team.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:12.980
My role is more like helping protect and

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:14.330
and grow the business.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:18.380
I just want to help, you
know, build better businesses

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:19.790
around these brands.

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:21.410
A business model that everyone seems

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:22.970
to think is dying, right?

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:26.120
For sure. I've said like
I feel like I'm the person

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:28.790
running into a burning building.

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:31.130
I mean here's i-D,

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:34.340
I mean you own this
magazine now, can we just..

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.410
Not just this one for $12.99.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:39.710
No, I'm so proud of the fact

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:42.470
that I am continuing this legacy

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:46.380
Today,

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.440
Kloss is taking us to
New York City for a look

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:51.560
behind the cover of Bedford
Media, the company she founded

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:53.690
with her husband Josh Kushner.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:55.980
Bedford is built on a simple idea.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:57.690
Legacy magazines with decades

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.090
of cultural credibility are worth saving

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:02.730
Love a New York Street.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:04.110
Oh, this is a real one.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.730
What magazines were you reading as a kid?

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:08.880
I, I don't think I was reading

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:10.380
magazines until I was in them.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:11.910
I mean you were thirteen

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:13.050
when you started?

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:14.520
So I guess yeah,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.700
but I, you know for me
like magazines are the

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.200
platform to tell stories.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.860
Yeah, to show ideas, to
showcase people. Yeah.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:26.010
It's not just to sort of
continue to chug along something

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:27.150
that I'm nostalgic about,

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:30.540
but actually we consume more stories

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:31.830
and information than ever.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:34.740
I was thinking a lot about the Life photo

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.030
archive, which is iconic.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.860
I mean space and

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.870
Oh. War. I mean American history,

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:41.010
civil rights, right?

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.260
Like there's some photos
of you in there. Yes.

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:44.880
What are the possibilities there?

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.470
The archive is incredible.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:49.860
Life was really the platform
that showed what was happening

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.980
around the world before we
lived in such a globally

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.290
connected, digitally immersive world.

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:59.790
And so a photograph told a
whole story about a person,

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:02.490
about a war, about what was happening

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.280
that you might not see in your day to day.

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:08.100
And I think that to me like
that resonates so much.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.400
I mean it's such a privilege
to, to take a brand like that

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.710
and to think about how to
bring it to life today.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.660
What is Life moving forward?

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:17.760
Well, Life Magazine,
you'll have to wait and see.

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:22.680
While the future of life
is still taking shape.

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:24.930
i-D Magazine is up

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.750
and running in the heart
of the Lower East Side.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:30.750
There's a lot of things that
are in the works right now.

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:34.680
We just launched our spring
issue. Fashion month madness just

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.020
happened and they like covered it all.

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:38.850
Come meet everybody. Yeah.

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:40.860
Hi
This is Emily.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:42.450
Thanks for having us.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:45.120
Wait, I love these mics though. These

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.425
are, who runs around with
these kind of? All of us.

00:10:48.425 --> 00:10:50.225
Yeah.

00:10:50.225 --> 00:10:52.020
Is this like man or
woman on the street kind

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:53.340
of a thing? Like this is,

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:54.340
Yeah.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:55.745
Hi i-D, hi i-D, hi i-D.

00:10:55.745 --> 00:10:57.900
Hi i-d, I'm Sarah and
I'm at the Prada show.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:00.180
Hi i-D, my name is Bhavitha Mandava.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:01.717
We cover it all.
Yes, I love it. It's

00:11:01.717 --> 00:11:02.993
true, the

00:11:02.993 --> 00:11:06.900
pulse. What is i-D's point of view?

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:11.640
Trish and Terry Jones founded
i-D. Terry Jones literally

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:14.310
hand stapled the magazines himself

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:16.830
and he invented something
called the Straight Up,

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:20.580
which is the modern day sort
of street style photography.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:25.350
The media landscape has totally changed.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:27.630
There's digital, there's print,

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:30.030
there's experiential, there's merch.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.680
What's the overall strategy?

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:35.490
I think like to continue
to innovate within all

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.530
of these spaces and reach
people where they are.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.560
I don't think that we're gonna
have like Facebook outcomes

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:42.480
on this investment necessarily,

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.730
but like that's not my intention here.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:48.840
Like my intention is to build
a really viable business

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:51.600
but to make really meaningful
work along the way.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:54.760
Maybe you're not looking
for Facebook outcomes,

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.530
but like what do you see

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:59.440
as the real business
opportunity in an industry

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:01.810
that has very challenging economics?

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:03.940
When we bought this business
out of bankruptcy, we had

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:06.100
to fully rebuild all pieces of it.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.710
The commercial business,
the creative agency piece,

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:11.590
the editorial team. The last
two years have been sort

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:14.410
of rebuilding the core business

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:17.260
and that really has
been part of this thesis

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:21.010
with Bedford is like how can we build

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:23.950
and run our website without
a hundred person tech team?

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:26.380
Like how can we run

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:29.500
and build, you know, all parts
of the business in a leaner,

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:31.390
scrappier, startup way?

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:35.230
And that candidly is part of
the advantage we have to a lot

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:37.360
