Post, Elon Musk with X.

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:14.020
There's a lot of anxiety
about editorial independence.

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:16.330
Who do you think is doing it right

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:19.960
and what worries you the most?

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:21.700
To me like

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:24.730
that's been very clear from
the start is like our editorial

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:27.190
team has editorial independence.

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:29.530
I think it's like, the
intention, you know,

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:31.810
for me really comes from like

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.090
how can I support this artist colony?

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:36.670
And not just in a philanthropic way,

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:39.850
but like actually how
can I push it to grow

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:43.030
and keep getting, you know,
smarter with the times

00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:44.900
Curating i-D's

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:47.170
soul with Kloss is Steff Yotka

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:49.360
i-D's global editorial director.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:51.520
She's tasked with keeping
the magazine on the cutting

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:53.200
edge of youth culture.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:56.050
What is Karlie's role day to
day like? How involved is she?

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:57.310
Every other job I've had,

00:13:57.310 --> 00:14:00.040
the person in her role
is like a old white guy.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.260
And so it's great to be able

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:04.600
to have these conversations
about the present

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:08.230
and the future of i-D with
someone who isn't like stymied

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:10.840
by a conservative view
of what media should be.

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:13.690
Everyone has content
everywhere at their fingertips.

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:14.980
Why do we need magazines?

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:19.390
Because we love them. We could
have just made an Instagram

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:20.770
or we could have just made a TikTok,

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:23.380
but I think the reason to
have a print magazine is

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:25.840
that it does mean it's forever.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:27.730
It will never be deleted
from the internet.

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:30.100
It is a physical object that is a record

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:32.320
of what we thought was cool.

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:34.630
Kloss isn't just trying to disrupt media,

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:37.540
she's also trying to expand
who gets a seat at the table in

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:39.130
tech?

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:41.560
Apply to Kode with Klossy.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:45.400
In 2015 she launched Kode with
Klossy, a free coding camp

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:49.090
for girls because who's
in the room matters. I am

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:50.090
one.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:52.280
What do you think is the value

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:54.110
of reaching girls at that age?

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:57.440
Like 13 to 18? Yeah. Which
is the Kode with Klossy age.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:58.970
Yeah. There's so many studies

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:01.550
around why girls self-select out in math

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:03.920
and decide that they're not good at math

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:07.910
or STEM topics broadly
long before 13 years old.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:12.020
We are creating a space
to come participate in

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:15.080
like the creative applications of code.

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:19.580
Especially girls that are not given access

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:22.280
to these tools or they
don't think it's for them.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:28.585
I'm so proud of our
12,000 alum, just over 70%

00:15:28.585 --> 00:15:30.020
of them who go into college major

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:31.910
or minor in computer science.

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:34.610
What I care about is that
there are girls and women

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:38.240
and people of all sorts of
lived experience helping inform

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:40.130
that technology at all levels.

